WEBVTT

00:01.300 --> 00:02.165
SeaQuery.

00:02.485 --> 00:06.630
A metric-conditioned vision-language framework for underwater ecological monitoring.

00:09.230 --> 00:12.134
Underwater robots can now survey coral reefs at scale.

00:12.454 --> 00:16.078
The bottleneck is turning those images into ecological information.

00:16.398 --> 00:18.770
Segmentation gives pixel-level measurements,

00:18.770 --> 00:20.442
but it cannot answer questions.

00:20.762 --> 00:25.039
Vision-language models, or VLMs, answer questions in natural language,

00:25.039 --> 00:27.116
but give no quantitative evidence.

00:29.220 --> 00:30.905
SeaQuery combines both.

00:31.225 --> 00:34.936
A SegFormer model measures five indicators: live, bleached,

00:34.936 --> 00:38.585
and dead coral cover, algae, and crown-of-thorns starfish.

00:38.905 --> 00:41.628
These measurements can drive an explicit decision rule.

00:41.947 --> 00:45.704
Or they can condition the VLM, either as a text prefix,

00:45.704 --> 00:46.934
called SeaQuery-P,

00:47.254 --> 00:49.896
or as metric embeddings inserted at a verified,

00:49.896 --> 00:52.481
backbone-specific position, called SeaQuery-F.

00:54.580 --> 00:57.486
We tested nine model variants under five conditions.

00:57.806 --> 01:00.266
No metrics, or adaptive or full metrics,

01:00.266 --> 01:02.295
delivered by prefix or by fusion.

01:02.615 --> 01:08.609
That gives 45 configurations on 5,158 CoralVQA questions,

01:08.929 --> 01:13.570
plus a reef-state test on all 392 Coralscapes test images.

01:15.670 --> 01:18.381
So, do the metrics help? Without any training,

01:18.381 --> 01:21.328
they improved exact match for four of five models.

01:21.648 --> 01:25.535
LLaVA gained the most, from 37.4 to 60.2 percent.

01:25.855 --> 01:27.746
PaliGemma2 Mix was the exception.

01:27.746 --> 01:30.038
Every conditioned setting made it worse.

01:32.140 --> 01:34.171
How the metrics are delivered also matters,

01:34.171 --> 01:36.108
and the best choice depends on the model.

01:36.428 --> 01:38.281
LLaVA prefers the text prefix,

01:38.281 --> 01:42.233
while BLIP-2 and PaliGemma2 Mix prefer fusion with full metrics.

01:42.553 --> 01:46.123
Choosing adaptive or full context is just as model-dependent.

01:46.443 --> 01:50.404
Conditioning is a model interface to validate, not a universal fix.

01:52.500 --> 01:54.952
Conditioning adds context at inference.

01:54.952 --> 01:59.289
What if the model itself is trained on the task? Low-rank adaptation,

01:59.289 --> 02:03.124
or LoRA, trained on 40,000 questions, gave the largest gains.

02:03.444 --> 02:06.926
Every adapted model improved by 27 to 39 points.

02:07.246 --> 02:10.865
InternVL2.5 reached 74.25 percent exact match,

02:10.865 --> 02:15.191
the best of all 45 configurations, without any metrics.

02:17.290 --> 02:23.347
So how does conditioning compare with training? Conditioning alone took LLaVA to 60.2 percent,

02:23.347 --> 02:26.697
close to its LoRA adapter at 67.2, with no training.

02:27.017 --> 02:29.681
That recovers about three quarters of the LoRA gain.

02:30.001 --> 02:31.139
After adaptation,

02:31.139 --> 02:35.356
extra metrics became neutral or harmful for LLaVA and InternVL,

02:35.356 --> 02:37.899
and gave PaliGemma2 Mix a small boost.

02:40.000 --> 02:43.119
A LLaVA adapter trained with metrics scored only 8 percent.

02:43.439 --> 02:47.291
The cause was not the metrics. It kept talking past the answer,

02:47.291 --> 02:49.615
which explained 85 percent of the gap.

02:49.935 --> 02:54.169
Teaching the model where to stop raised both adapters to about 67 percent.

02:54.489 --> 02:57.630
Then, metric training helped when metrics were given at inference,

02:57.630 --> 02:58.962
and hurt when they were not.

03:01.060 --> 03:04.066
These answers rely on the measurements themselves.

03:04.066 --> 03:05.388
How reliable are they?

03:05.708 --> 03:08.436
On 392 held-out Coralscapes images,

03:08.436 --> 03:12.958
live coral and algae agree closely with expert annotation.

03:13.278 --> 03:16.295
Bleaching agrees less, and dead coral is the weakest.

03:16.615 --> 03:19.512
Starfish counts match on 384 images,

03:19.512 --> 03:22.408
but only 14 images contain starfish.

03:24.510 --> 03:27.883
Beyond question answering, we tested reef-state decisions.

03:27.883 --> 03:31.722
An explicit rule on live coral and bleaching defines three states.

03:32.042 --> 03:34.004
Errors are weighted by consequence.

03:34.004 --> 03:36.638
Calling a degraded reef healthy costs the most.

03:38.740 --> 03:44.031
Applying this rule to segmentation gives 90.8 percent accuracy and the lowest cost.

03:44.351 --> 03:48.496
Among the VLMs, BLIP-2 with fusion is the most accurate,

03:48.496 --> 03:49.680
at 41.1 percent.

03:50.001 --> 03:52.291
But accuracy is not safety.

03:52.291 --> 03:58.057
BLIP-2 calls 104 of 160 degraded reefs healthy, the costliest error.

03:58.377 --> 04:02.381
LLaVA is less accurate, but makes half as many of these errors,

04:02.381 --> 04:04.161
and has the lowest VLM cost.

04:04.161 --> 04:07.657
Even always answering degraded costs less than any VLM.

04:07.977 --> 04:10.879
Segmentation calls only one degraded reef healthy.

04:12.980 --> 04:16.271
Why do the VLMs struggle? The raw answers show it.

04:16.271 --> 04:18.641
LLaVA picks option A for every reef,

04:18.641 --> 04:22.722
and the adapted PaliGemma2 Mix answers healthy for every reef.

04:23.042 --> 04:27.021
Segmentation stays lowest-cost across all 40 cost settings tested.

04:27.341 --> 04:31.760
And turning a degraded reef healthy needs a measurement error of at least 20 points,

04:31.760 --> 04:33.812
larger than the 95th-percentile errors.

04:35.910 --> 04:40.478
Finally, what does this mean for deployment? On a server GPU,

04:40.478 --> 04:44.821
the segmentation decision takes 79 milliseconds per image.

04:44.821 --> 04:48.490
Adding a VLM decision brings the pipeline to 166.

04:48.810 --> 04:50.904
So measurements can run on every frame,

04:50.904 --> 04:52.891
with the VLM called only when needed.

04:54.990 --> 04:55.732
In conclusion,

04:55.732 --> 04:58.751
ecological context can improve visual question answering,

04:58.751 --> 05:01.505
but the gain depends on the model and the interface.

05:01.825 --> 05:05.542
LoRA gives larger gains, and changes the value of that context.

05:05.862 --> 05:07.591
For rule-defined reef decisions,

05:07.591 --> 05:10.400
explicit logic on measurements is far more reliable.

05:10.400 --> 05:14.181
Part of this is by design, since the labels use the same measurements.

05:16.280 --> 05:18.209
Three steps remain for future work.

05:18.529 --> 05:18.849
First,

05:18.849 --> 05:23.167
the injected metrics need validation on the question-answering images themselves,

05:23.167 --> 05:24.766
which have no reference masks.

05:25.086 --> 05:27.800
Second, our measurements come from Red Sea imagery,

05:27.800 --> 05:29.928
so other reefs and cameras need testing.

05:30.248 --> 05:32.683
Third, single images should give way to video,

05:32.683 --> 05:34.906
to check consistency across survey frames.

05:37.010 --> 05:37.658
Thank you.
